bevy-horizon: let loads of one kind share a scoreboard (NAK_DEBUG=share)

For mesa-switch (https://github.com/danfromtico/mesa-switch, by danfromtico and NaGaa95; MIT)
at 606441c9, after 0001-0022. scripts/build_nvk.sh applies it; this text before the first
"diff --git" line is ignored by git apply.

Maxwell has six scoreboards for variable-latency operations. A texture fetch or memory load
usually takes two: one that its sources have been read, one that its result has been written.
When all six are taken, NAK makes the next one wait for the oldest, so a run of loads whose
results are used after the run has only about three in flight. In sk8's cluster test the 4th,
5th and 6th of 24 texel reads wait for the results of the 1st, 2nd and 3rd.

A scoreboard is a counter, though, and a wait on it covers everything it holds. Mesa's older
Maxwell compiler relies on that: when it runs out it puts the operation on scoreboard 5 anyway
(insertBarriers() in nv50_ir_emit_gm107.cpp), and its DEPBAR.LE has a 6-bit count. maxas found
that memory loads, which go out in order, share a scoreboard reliably, and that S2Rs, which can
be processed out of order, don't.

With NAK_DEBUG=share, a texture fetch or memory load that finds no free scoreboard (in the
cross-block pass, NAK_DEBUG=xbar) takes one held only by operations of its kind (texture
fetches, or loads from the same memory space) for the same purpose (reading or writing), at
most eight of them. Of those it takes the one first waited on latest. Anything else still
waits for the oldest. xbar_verify() checks the result under the counter model; if the check
fails, NAK keeps the old barriers and prints "NAK xbar: kept legacy barriers".

Compiled on the host with the Switch flags, sk8's cluster test kernels go from 165-231 waits to
148-194 with the same instructions, and a static count of loads in flight when each is issued
roughly doubles (1.4 to 2.6 in the old kernel, 2.2 to 4.4 in the new one). The triangle cull
goes from 1.3 to 2.7; the world shader barely changes (1.41 to 1.49).

Only the GPU can show whether this is right, because Ryujinx ignores the scheduling control
codes. So it is off by default, in Switch builds too. Four unit tests cover runs of loads, the
cap of eight, reads and writes kept apart, and different kinds kept apart.

